    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The COVID-19 pandemic raised the global prevalence of depressive and anxiety\r\nsymptoms in children and teenagers. Therefore, there is an urgent need to provide\r\nteenagers with coping tools and support to improve their mental health. Research over\r\nthe past two decades suggests that mindfulness is effective in addressing depression\r\nand anxiety. We believe that digital technology might allow us to create a learning\r\nexperience that promotes daily mindfulness practices and a sense of belonging in\r\nteenagers and young adults.\r\nMindful Circles is a mobile app for teenagers to practice mindfulness with the support of\r\npeers and experienced mentors remotely. Our project empowers teenagers to become\r\nmindfulness practitioners and helps them incorporate mindfulness techniques not just to\r\ncope with stress, loneliness, and depression into their daily lives but to create a\r\nconsistent routine as a proactive measure to increase body and emotional awareness.\r\nDiary studies have demonstrated that Mindful Circles successfully supports our\r\nlearners’ ability to feel safe, apply new mindfulness techniques, and build confidence in\r\nthe practice, all in the context of a community.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Quiroz, M. (2022). Mindful Circles Project Report. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/mn482gq3212. https://doi.org/10.25740/mn482gq3212.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
